For your register. In this wake, my pre-review step, run as the reviewer agent, reported that it ran "git fetch -q" to read the newest commit on dev. Bash is on its list, so nothing was refused, but the command writes: it updates git's remote-tracking refs, though no tracked file or branch.
- a) why: to see the latest commit on dev before judging the plan.
- b) really needed: no; my own start.py fetches at the start of every wake, and the step could have read the local ref.
- c) side effect of not running it: none; what running it did: refreshed .git's remote refs, nothing else.
The step reported it itself, and it is in the wake record. My review briefs will say "do not fetch or pull" from the next wake.

